    "media:imagetext": "New York\nJanuary 25, 1954\nDear Familys\nJust back from my 31st crossing of the Atlantic and thought you might be\ninterested in some of the highlights of this safari. On the 17th received a cable\nfrom Germany that a deal which I have been concerned with for some years might go\nthrough or fall through, dependent on whether I came over. That was attaching more\nimportance than warranted, but nevertheless with a great deal of regret I cancelled\nout my participation in a weekend trip which Hebe and Frank had planned to Great\nBarrington and got myself booked on the President Special, Friday the 15th.\nCouldn't 'phone Debby so hen she thought I was on a trip to Massachusetts I was\nin fact out over the Atlantic. It was the fastest trip I've made - only 10 1/2\nhours from New York to London. The President Sp cial is an experiment of PAA's in\nsurcharged luxury: Meals by Maxim's of Paris, a red carpet out to the airplane, four\nstewardesses - count 'em, four - orchids for the ladies, champagne but good and berths\nfor about half the passengers, with \"sleeperette\" chairs that lie almost flat for the\nrest.\nI met an old friend from Manila days, and after we took off at 4830 P.M. we\ntalked until dinner. After dinner we adjourned to the lower deck lounge and yak-\nyakked until midnight, New York time. Then I realized that werd be in London at 3:00\nA. Mo, New York time and he had bought a $25.00 berth, so chased him to bed. We had\na couple of hours sleep and then they called us for breakfast at 2800 A.M., New York\nbut 7:00 A.M. London time. That's one bad feature about a quick trip eastbound - you\ndon't get any sleep!\nAt London I transferred to a British European Airways Elizabethan Class twin-\nengined plane for Amsterdam and Hamburg. The \"Elizabethan\" part was no doubt correct\nbut the \"Class\" was distinctly lacking. The interior looked like a cross between a\nbarn and a hen house. A single stewardess devoted her full attention to selling\ncigarettes, beer and hard liquor. The lunch was typically British - cold and unin-",
